2.2 State whether each statement is True or False. If false, explain why.\par
\par
a) Comments cause the computer to print the text after the // on the screen when the program executes.\par
\par
Answer: FALSE.\par
\par
Explanation: Comments are ignored by the compiler and do not appear on the screen when the program executes.\par
\par
b) All variables must be given a type when they're declared.\par
\par
Answer: TRUE.\par
\par
Explanation: In Java, a variable must have a declared data type, such as int, double, or String.\par
\par
c) Java considers the variables number and NuMbEr to be identical.\par
\par
Answer: FALSE.\par
\par
Explanation: Java is case-sensitive. Therefore, number and NuMbEr are different variables.\par
\par
d) The remainder operator (%) can be used only with integer operands.\par
\par
Answer: FALSE.\par
\par
Explanation: The remainder operator can also be used with floating-point operands, such as double values.\par
\par
e) The arithmetic operators *, /, %, + and - all have the same level of precedence.\par
\par
Answer: FALSE.\par
\par
Explanation: Multiplication (*), division (/) and remainder (%) have higher precedence than addition (+) and subtraction (-).\par
\par
