2.30 Separating the Digits in an Integer\par
\par
Question: Write an application that accepts a five-digit integer and prints its individual digits separated by three spaces.\par
\par
Answer\par
\par
import java.util.Scanner;\par
\par
public class SeparateDigits \{\par
    public static void main(String[] args) \{\par
\par
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\par
\par
        System.out.print("Enter a five-digit integer: ");\par
        int number = input.nextInt();\par
\par
        System.out.printf("%d   ", number / 10000);\par
        System.out.printf("%d   ", (number / 1000) % 10);\par
        System.out.printf("%d   ", (number / 100) % 10);\par
        System.out.printf("%d   ", (number / 10) % 10);\par
        System.out.printf("%d%n", number % 10);\par
\par
        input.close();\par
    \}\par
\}\par
\par
Example output\par
\par
Enter a five-digit integer: 42339\par
4   2   3   3   9\par
\par
Explanation:\par
\par
The program uses division and the remainder operator to extract each digit.\par
\par
For example, given 42339:\par
\par
42339 / 10000 gives 4.\par
\par
(42339 / 1000) % 10 gives 2.\par
\par
(42339 / 100) % 10 gives 3.\par
\par
(42339 / 10) % 10 gives 3.\par
\par
42339 % 10 gives 9.\par
\par
\par
What happens if you enter more than five digits?\par
\par
The program will not correctly display all the digits as five separate digits. The first calculation may also produce a number greater than 9.\par
\par
What happens if you enter fewer than five digits?\par
\par
The program will still print five positions, adding leading zeros where necessary. For example, 123 produces 0   0   1   2   3.\par
\par
