2.33 Body Mass Index (BMI) Calculator\par
\par
Question: Create a BMI calculator that reads the user's weight and height, calculates BMI, and displays the BMI categories.\par
\par
Your document allows either pounds and inches or kilograms and meters. We'll use kilograms and meters.\par
\par
The formula is:\par
\par
\\[\par
BMI = \\frac\{\\text\{weight in kilograms\}\}\{(\\text\{height in meters\})^2\}\par
\\]\par
\par
Answer\par
\par
import java.util.Scanner;\par
\par
public class BMICalculator \{\par
    public static void main(String[] args) \{\par
\par
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\par
\par
        System.out.print("Enter weight in kilograms: ");\par
        double weight = input.nextDouble();\par
\par
        System.out.print("Enter height in meters: ");\par
        double height = input.nextDouble();\par
\par
        if (weight <= 0 || height <= 0) \{\par
            System.out.println("Weight and height must be positive.");\par
        \} else \{\par
            double bmi = weight / (height * height);\par
\par
            System.out.printf("Your BMI is: %.2f%n", bmi);\par
\par
            System.out.println("\\nBMI VALUES");\par
            System.out.println("Underweight: less than 18.5");\par
            System.out.println("Normal: 18.5 to less than 25");\par
            System.out.println("Overweight: 25 to less than 30");\par
            System.out.println("Obese: 30 or greater");\par
        \}\par
\par
        input.close();\par
    \}\par
\}\par
\par
Example output\par
\par
Enter weight in kilograms: 60\par
Enter height in meters: 1.7\par
Your BMI is: 20.76\par
\par
BMI VALUES\par
Underweight: less than 18.5\par
Normal: 18.5 to less than 25\par
Overweight: 25 to less than 30\par
Obese: 30 or greater\par
\par
Explanation:\par
\par
The program divides the weight by the height multiplied by itself.\par
\par
For example:\par
\par
\\[\par
BMI = \\frac\{60\}\{1.7 \\times 1.7\} \\approx 20.76\par
\\]\par
\par
Important: BMI is a screening measure, not a complete assessment of someone's health. For children and teenagers, BMI needs to be interpreted using age- and sex-specific percentiles rather than adult categories.\par
