Answer\par
\par
import java.util.Scanner;\par
\par
public class PopulationGrowth \{\par
    public static void main(String[] args) \{\par
\par
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\par
\par
        System.out.print("Enter current world population: ");\par
        double population = input.nextDouble();\par
\par
        System.out.print("Enter annual growth rate (%): ");\par
        double growthRate = input.nextDouble();\par
\par
        double rate = growthRate / 100.0;\par
\par
        for (int year = 1; year <= 5; year++) \{\par
            population = population * (1 + rate);\par
\par
            System.out.printf(\par
                "Population after %d year(s): %.0f%n",\par
                year, population\par
            );\par
        \}\par
\par
        input.close();\par
    \}\par
\}\par
\par
Example\par
\par
Suppose you enter a hypothetical population of 1000000 and a growth rate of 2.\par
\par
The program calculates:\par
\par
Year 1: 1,020,000\par
\par
Year 2: 1,040,400\par
\par
Year 3: 1,061,208\par
\par
Year 4: 1,082,432\par
\par
Year 5: 1,104,081\par
\par
\par
These are illustrative figures, not current world population estimates.\par
\par
Explanation:\par
\par
The formula used is:\par
\par
\\[\par
